1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:05,340
Module 1, Video 1.5: Why PAERA anchored.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,460
Yeah. So PAERA

3
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:10,720
gives your team a five-year head start. Right.

4
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:14,400
Like if you adopt it, your architecture work begins on day one.

5
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:19,300
Oh, wow. Otherwise, you know, your first year is basically spent inventing what is already published.

6
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:23,810
Well, when we talk about architectural work for a government, we're really talking about enterprise architecture, right?

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,680
Exactly. Like the agreed structural picture- Okay... of your government.

8
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:31,220
So if a ministry needs to draw that picture, um,

9
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:33,960
what are the actual options on the table?

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:40,210
You essentially have two paths. Okay. Path one is designing an EA framework for your country completely from scratch.

11
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:44,840
Right. And, uh, path two is anchoring on one that already exists.

12
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:47,800
I mean, path one just sounds like a massive administrative bottleneck.

13
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:51,790
Mm. What is the actual cost and time before we see any usable architecture?

14
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:57,300
It is severe. I mean, you are looking at 12 to 18 months of hiring consultants just to lay the groundwork.

15
00:00:57,340 --> 00:00:59,040
Wait, a year and a half just for groundwork?

16
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:07,390
Yeah, yeah. You are paying them to define basic terminology and, you know, debate principles before a single architect draws any picture of any ministry.

17
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:10,480
So you're just spending all that time stuck in semantic debates.

18
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:12,620
Right. Which brings us to path two,

19
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:17,460
anchoring on PAERA, the Public Administration Ecosystem

20
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:19,400
Reference Architecture.

21
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:22,390
And that was published in 2024 under GovStack, right?

22
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:29,470
Correct. And because it is built explicitly for the public sector use case, well, that groundwork is already done, so the work begins on day one.

23
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:32,640
Exactly. But, um, I wanna push back here a little.

24
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:35,740
What specific groundwork are we actually avoiding paying for?

25
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:39,360
Like, what tangible things does PAERA hand a team on day one?

26
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:44,570
Well, there are five specific things. First, a defined way to organize ministries and authorities.

27
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:45,420
Okay. How so?

28
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:49,780
So instead of your team arguing for three months over how to classify a government body,

29
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:52,100
the classification is already published.

30
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:59,150
Oh, so you know immediately if a body is a policy unit, a regulatory agency, or like a service delivery authority.

31
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:01,890
Exactly, which saves endless committee meetings.

32
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:08,220
Right. But how do you ensure all those different authorities are actually speaking the same language once they're classified?

33
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:13,720
Well, that is the second thing. You get plain words for basic terms defined on day one.

34
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:14,680
Like what kind of terms?

35
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:18,620
Uh, terms like capability, service, application, and data domain.

36
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:20,680
The vocabulary is just completely settled.

37
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:22,410
That makes sense. And the third?

38
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,480
Third is a library of common building blocks.

39
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:32,900
Oh, right. Yeah. We are talking about foundational elements like identity, payments, information sharing, and registers.

40
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:36,130
Wait, really? So if my ministry needs a secure payment system,

41
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:39,530
my architects don't have to design the underlying structure?

42
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:43,240
No, they don't. These come with published specifications and working examples.

43
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:43,960
You just use them.

44
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:44,940
Wow, that's huge.

45
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:53,020
It is. Fourth, PAERA gives you 10 architectural principles, covering everything from the rule of law to user-centered design.

46
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:55,780
And I assume those are already heavily debated globally.

47
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:02,520
Heavily debated. So your team's job is simply to extend them for your country's context, not draft them from a blank page.

48
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:04,020
Which is much faster.

49
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:05,780
Absolutely. And fifth

50
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:14,799
is a way to run the work. Okay. PAERO provides a lifecycle discover, assess, adapt, plan, execute, and govern.

51
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:21,820
Got it. And it comes with all the required roles and sign-offs already mapped out per phase, which we will actually walk through in the next video.

52
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:25,760
Okay, so that makes sense for getting an initial sector off the ground.

53
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:29,800
But, um, what happens when a government wants to expand?

54
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:31,240
What do you mean?

55
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:34,200
Like, let's say we establish this for the Ministry of Education.

56
00:03:34,829 --> 00:03:42,000
Uh-huh. When we move to health or social protection or agriculture, are we forced to start from scratch because those sectors are fundamentally different?

57
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:45,320
Ah. That is a common concern, but the answer is no.

58
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:48,420
PAERA works seamlessly across all those sectors.

59
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:54,970
Wait, really? Yeah. Building the enterprise architecture muscle once means every next sector reuses that exact same investment.

60
00:03:55,410 --> 00:03:57,880
The framework is not rebought or recustomized.

61
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,359
So the second sector is inherently cheaper to integrate.

62
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:02,120
Right, and the third is cheaper still.

63
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:08,260
Wow. But beyond just having a static framework, what active resources does this actually connect your ministry to?

64
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:11,530
Well, adopting PAERA plugs you into a working network.

65
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:19,250
Your team gets access to the building block specifications, GovMarket, which offers implementations that are already compliance checked.

66
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:24,820
Oh, nice. Yeah. Plus a prototyping sandbox to safely test integrations and a shared knowledge base.

67
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:27,520
Ultimately, PAERA is not a vendor choice.

68
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:35,060
Right. It is the choice to start with the work other countries have already done, so your team spends its time on what is specific to your country.

69
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:36,480
Sources are in the video description.
